<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirabai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1498–c. 1547</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Meera Bai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirabai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> princess and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mystic poet of the bhakti movement, devoted to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. Born into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> family of Merta, she is traditionally said to have married Bhoj <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> of Mewar, and after his early death to have rejected the conventions of a royal widow to live as a devotee. Hundreds of devotional songs attributed to her are still sung across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabai.jpg" alt="Mirabai" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kangra_painting_of_Mirabai,_the_female_Bhakti_saint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mirabai was born into a Rathore Rajput royal family at Kudki, in the present Beawar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, and spent her childhood at Merta. She was the daughter of Ratan Singh Rathore and the granddaughter of Rao Dudaji of Merta, which made her a great-granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a>. In 1516 she had an arranged marriage to Bhoj Raj, the crown prince of Mewar.</p><p>He was wounded in a war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> in 1518 and died of his wounds in 1521. Both her father and her father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> died a few days after their defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a>. After Rana Sanga, Vikram Singh became ruler of Mewar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Her poems, often sung as bhajans, are lyrical padas in the Rajasthani language and describe love for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, separation from him and dissatisfaction with the world. She often calls him Girdhar, the mountain lifter, and speaks of him as her only true husband. In poems of separation she sometimes portrays him as a detached yogi who leaves lovers in agony, and she presents herself as a yogini.</p><p>The earliest datable reference to her is a poem of praise in Nabhadas's Bhaktamal, composed about 1600. Millions of hymns are attributed to her in the Indian tradition, but scholars think only a few hundred authentic, and only two poems have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> older than the early 18th century. Poets who admired her probably composed many of the rest.</p><p>Poems attributed to her also appear in a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a> made in 1604, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus">Sikh gurus</a> did not include in the standard edition, and in the Prem Ambodh Pothi of 1693.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The legends of her life vary widely. In the best known, her in-laws tried to kill her with poison sent as nectar and with a basket holding a snake, and she was saved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. Scholars doubt a story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a> visited her, because Tansen joined Akbar's court 15 years after her death. The three oldest records that mention her all date from the 17th century.</p><p>Nancy Martin-Kershaw suggests that any persecution she met was likelier to come from political chaos and conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> than from religious or social convention. Tradition holds that she spent her last years at Dwarka or Vrindavan and disappeared into an idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> in 1547 after being poisoned by her brother-in-law, which scholars contest for lack of evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirabai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet-saint <b>Mirabai</b>, whose devotional songs to Krishna are sung across India, is traditionally said to have married into the ruling house as the wife of Rana Sanga’s son Bhoj Raj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tamil poet-saints, the Alvars devoted to Vishnu and the Nayanars devoted to Shiva, composed hymns from the 6th to the 9th centuries that launched the bhakti movement, which later spread north through poets such as <b>Mirabai</b> and Tulsidas.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Vallabha taught, and Surdas and <b>Mirabai</b> sang, devotion to Krishna, and in 1574 Tulsidas began the Ramcharitmanas.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Dwarka, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.24,68.97&amp;year=1529" aria-label="Dwarka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Mirabai"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5707 5634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5707 5634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=22.24,68.97&amp;year=1529">What was here in 1529? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
